"""Science B4/B5 Weeks 2–12: authored notes and assessment cards.
Week 1 is imported from the first batch to make complete subject books.
"""
from pathlib import Path
import sys,copy
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'term1-batch1'/'source'))
from starter_content import DATA as STARTERS
LESSONS={}
for g in (4,5):
    m=copy.deepcopy(STARTERS[(g,'Science')]);m.update(week=1,grade=g,mapping_note='Matches the supplied Week 1 indicator.',source_codes=m['codes'])
    LESSONS[(g,1)]=m

def add(g,w,title,codes,source_codes,mapping_note,activity,safety,raw):
    cards=[]
    for line in raw.strip().splitlines():
        fields=line.split('|');assert len(fields)==6,(g,w,line)
        answer,fact,q2,wrong,reason,extend=fields
        cards.append(dict(answer=answer,fact=fact,q1='Which term best matches this description: '+fact[0].lower()+fact[1:]+'?',q2=q2,wrong=wrong.split(';'),reason=reason,extend=extend))
    assert len(cards)==10 and all(len(c['wrong'])==3 for c in cards)
    LESSONS[(g,w)]=dict(grade=g,week=w,title=title,codes=codes,source_codes=source_codes,mapping_note=mapping_note,activity=activity,safety=safety,cards=cards,page=24 if g==4 else 26)

add(4,2,'Animal life processes: movement, nutrition and reproduction','B4.1.1.1.2','B4.1.1.1.2','Direct indicator match; feeding categories provide supporting vocabulary.',
'Use pictures of a goat, hen, cat and butterfly. Record movement, food and young in a table. Ask learners to distinguish moving a body part from changing location. Construct simple parent–young matching cards and justify dietary classifications using examples.',
'Use photographs or observe familiar animals at a distance. Do not feed, chase or handle unfamiliar animals.',
'''Movement|A change in the position of a whole organism or one of its body parts|A goat turns its head while standing in one place. Which life activity is shown?|Germination;Condensation;Rusting|Movement can involve just one body part. Turning a head is movement even if the animal stays in the same place.|Explain why the goat has moved even without changing its location.
Locomotion|Movement of an organism from one place to another|A hen walks from its coop to a water bowl. What kind of movement is this?|Digestion only;Respiration only;Excretion only|The entire animal changes location. Movement of a wing without changing place would not by itself be locomotion.|Distinguish moving a body part from changing the location of the whole animal.
Nutrition|Obtaining and using food materials needed for life|A goat eats grass to obtain materials and energy. Which life process is involved?|Reproduction;Condensation;Pollination|Food supplies materials for growth and repair and energy for activities. Eating is part of nutrition, not the only event in it.|Give two reasons animals need food.
Herbivore|An animal whose diet consists mainly of plant material|A cow feeds mainly on grass. Which dietary group fits it?|Carnivore;Omnivore;Producer|The diet, rather than the animal’s size, supports the classification. Grass is plant material that provides nutrients.|Explain why the cow belongs to this dietary group.
Carnivore|An animal whose diet consists mainly of other animals|A lion eats mainly animal flesh. Which dietary group fits it?|Herbivore;Producer;Photosynthetic organism|The main food source is other animals. Classifying diet is different from judging whether the animal is dangerous.|Explain the evidence for placing a lion in this group.
Omnivore|An animal that normally eats both plant and animal material|A hen eats grains and insects. Which dietary group fits it?|Herbivore only;Carnivore only;Producer|Grains are plant material and insects are animal material. Both are represented in the diet.|Use the hen’s foods to justify its dietary classification.
Reproduction|The biological process by which organisms produce new individuals|An adult animal produces young of its own kind. Which life process is shown?|Evaporation;Weathering;Condensation|New individuals continue the group over generations. Not every individual must reproduce to be alive.|Explain why reproduction matters to the continuation of a group of animals.
Offspring|The young produced by a parent or parents|What is the general term for a goat’s kid in relation to its parents?|Habitat;Predator;Shelter|A kid is a young goat. It resembles its parents in many features but need not look identical in every detail.|Describe one similarity and one possible difference between an offspring and its parent.
Egg-laying|A reproductive pattern in which young develop in eggs laid outside the parent’s body|A hen lays eggs from which chicks may hatch. Which reproductive pattern is shown?|Seed production;Budding of leaves;Condensation|A developing chick can hatch from a fertilised egg under suitable conditions. Not every egg contains a developing chick.|Explain why laying an egg does not guarantee that it will hatch into a chick.
Live birth|A reproductive pattern in which developed young emerge alive from the mother|A goat gives birth to a kid rather than laying an egg. Which pattern is shown?|Egg-laying;Seed dispersal;Pollination|Goats normally give birth to live young and feed them milk. Different animal groups have different reproductive patterns.|Compare the birth of a kid with the hatching of a chick.''')

add(4,3,'Plant forms: trees, shrubs, climbers and creepers','B4.1.1.2.2','B4.1.1.1.3','Source suffix is absent in local curriculum; plant-form description is taken from B4.1.1.2.2 within the scheduled living-things block.',
'Observe safe school plants or photographs. Draw a tree, shrub, climber and creeper. Label stem position and support. Sort new examples by visible features rather than memorised names; record any uncertain specimen for teacher discussion.',
'Do not taste plant parts or touch unknown sap, thorns or irritant leaves. Do not climb trees or pull down climbing plants.',
'''Tree|A woody plant usually having one main trunk and branches above the ground|A mango plant has a strong main trunk and a branching crown. Which plant form fits it?|Creeper;Climber;Small herb|A main woody trunk supports the crown. Height alone is not enough because young trees can be short.|Explain why a young mango plant can still be classified as a tree.
Shrub|A woody plant commonly having several stems branching near the ground|A hibiscus bush has several woody stems from close to the soil. Which form best fits it?|Creeper;Climber;Tree with one main trunk|Several low branches and woody stems support this classification. A shrub need not have the single main trunk typical of a tree.|Compare the stem arrangement of a shrub and a typical tree.
Climber|A plant with a stem that uses external support to grow upward|A bean vine grows upward around a stake. Which form is shown?|Free-standing tree;Creeper only;Woody shrub|The stem uses the stake for support rather than holding itself fully upright. Twining or tendrils may help attachment.|Explain the role of the stake in the bean vine’s growth.
Creeper|A plant whose weak stems spread mainly along the ground|A pumpkin plant spreads over the soil without climbing a support. Which form is shown?|Tree;Upright shrub;Tall woody trunk|Its stems spread horizontally and do not support a tall upright body. The growth habit, not the fruit’s size, decides the form.|Identify the feature that makes this plant a creeper.
Herb|A plant with relatively soft, non-woody stems|A small plant has soft green stems rather than persistent woody branches. Which description fits?|Woody tree;Woody shrub;Dry trunk|The stem texture is the key feature. The everyday meaning of a cooking herb is narrower than this botanical description.|Explain why stem structure is more useful than culinary use for this classification.
Trunk|The main woody stem of a typical tree|Which part of a mango tree supports its major branches?|Petal;Seed coat;Root hair|The trunk holds branches above the ground and contains pathways for transport. Roots have a different anchoring and absorbing role.|State two functions associated with a tree’s main stem.
Tendril|A slender plant structure that can coil around a support|A climbing plant grips a thin support with a curling structure. What is it called?|Seed coat;Petal;Storage root|Coiling helps attachment and support. The structure is not a root absorbing water from soil.|Explain how a tendril helps a climber reach upward.
Woody stem|A relatively hard, persistent stem containing wood|Which stem type helps trees and shrubs support their branches over many seasons?|Soft petal;Leaf blade;Seed coat|Wood contributes support and persistence. Both trees and shrubs can have woody stems despite different branching patterns.|Explain a shared stem feature of trees and shrubs.
Support|A structure that holds a climbing stem in a useful position|A gardener adds a trellis for a vine. What role does the trellis mainly serve?|Plant food;Seed embryo;Water source|The trellis provides mechanical support. It does not replace water, light or mineral nutrients needed by the plant.|Explain why a trellis cannot replace watering a climbing plant.
Growth habit|The characteristic way a plant’s stems grow and are arranged|A class compares plants that stand upright, climb or spread on the ground. Which feature are they comparing?|Flower colour only;Seed taste;Soil temperature only|Growth habit combines stem arrangement and support. Plant identification is stronger when observations are recorded before assigning labels.|Describe two observations useful when deciding whether a plant is a climber or creeper.''')

add(4,4,'Comparing the physical features of animal groups','B4.1.1.2.3','B4.1.1.1.4','Source suffix is absent locally; use the animal-feature description under B4.1.1.2.3. Includes amphibians as named in that description.',
'Compare labelled pictures of a goat, bird, lizard, butterfly and adult frog. Construct a feature matrix for covering, limbs, antennae and backbone. Use at least two features for difficult examples such as bats and snakes.',
'Use pictures and prepared models. Do not collect frogs, snakes, insects or unknown animal remains.',
'''Amphibian|An animal in a group including frogs, often with moist skin and a life cycle linked to water|An adult frog has moist skin and began life as an aquatic tadpole. Which group fits it?|Bird;Insect;Reptile|Many frogs change from aquatic larvae to adults that can spend time on land. The pattern differs from a lizard’s dry scaly covering.|Compare two features of a frog and a lizard.
Moist skin|A usually damp body covering characteristic of many amphibians|Which covering is commonly observed on a frog rather than a lizard?|Feathers;Dry scales;Dense fur|A frog’s skin must be protected from drying and damage. A lizard typically has a drier, scaly covering.|Explain why touching or drying a frog’s skin can be harmful.
Dry scales|A protective covering typical of reptiles such as lizards|Which covering helps identify a lizard among the pictured animals?|Feathers;Fur;Moist bare frog skin|Dry scales are useful reptile evidence when combined with other features. Scales alone do not separate reptiles from every other animal group.|Explain why more than one feature should be used in classification.
Six legs|The usual number of legs on an adult insect|Which feature distinguishes an adult butterfly from a spider?|Eight legs;Four walking legs;No body regions|Adult insects have three pairs of legs. Spiders have four pairs and are not insects, even though both groups include small crawling animals.|Explain why a spider should not be placed in the insect group.
Three body regions|The insect arrangement of head, thorax and abdomen|An ant drawing should show which main body arrangement?|One undivided trunk;Only head and tail;Four identical body regions|Head, thorax and abdomen have different roles. Legs attach to the thorax, so labels should reflect the actual arrangement.|Name the three regions and state where the legs attach.
Mammary glands|Structures in female mammals that produce milk for their young|A mother goat feeds her kid with milk produced by which structures?|Feathers;Antennae;Tendrils|Milk provides nourishment for the young. Together with hair, this supports mammalian classification more reliably than walking on four legs alone.|Explain why milk production is useful evidence for classifying a goat.
Feathers|A distinctive body covering identifying birds|A bat and a pigeon both fly. Which covering identifies the pigeon as a bird?|Hair;Moist bare skin;Dry reptile scales|The pigeon has feathers; the bat has hair and feeds its young milk. Shared flight does not mean the same animal group.|Explain why flight alone cannot separate birds from mammals.
Vertebrate|An animal possessing a backbone|A goat, lizard and frog all have which general classification?|Invertebrate;Insect;Arachnid|They have internal skeletons with backbones despite their different coverings. A butterfly lacks a backbone.|Explain a shared feature of these animals and contrast it with an insect.
Exoskeleton|An external supporting and protective covering|An insect’s firm outer covering is an example of what?|Backbone;Internal skull only;Feather coat|The covering supports and protects from outside. A vertebrate’s main supporting skeleton is internal.|Compare external and internal support using an insect and a goat.
Classification key|A sequence of feature-based choices used to identify an organism|A chart asks “feathers present?” and then offers further choices. What kind of identification tool is it?|Weather forecast;Food recipe;Calendar|Each choice should use observable, clear features. An ambiguous question such as “is it nice?” is not a sound scientific distinction.|Write one useful yes/no feature question for identifying an animal group.''')

add(4,5,'Materials and observable properties','Supporting material-property concepts; compare B5.1.2.1.1','B4.1.2.1.1','The cited B4 indicator is absent locally. This introductory properties unit is an explicit editorial interpretation of the scheduled Materials block; confirm with the school.',
'Use safe samples of paper, cloth, a plastic container, a wooden block and a smooth metal spoon. Observe texture, transparency, flexibility and water absorption. Record the property before suggesting a suitable use. Test one property at a time.',
'No sharp glass, electrical conductivity tests, tasting, heating or forceful bending. Clean water spills promptly.',
'''Hardness|Resistance of a material to indentation or scratching|A wooden block is harder to dent with gentle pressure than a sponge. Which property is compared?|Transparency;Colour;Absorption|Hardness concerns resistance to local deformation. A hard material may still fracture under impact; glass provides a familiar example.|Explain why a hard material is not necessarily unbreakable.
Softness|The ease with which a material yields under gentle pressure|A cushion compresses readily when pressed. Which property is useful here?|Transparency;Metallic shine;Waterproofing|A soft filling changes shape under pressure and provides comfort. The outer cover may need different properties such as strength.|Explain why a cushion may use different materials for its filling and cover.
Smooth texture|A surface with relatively few noticeable bumps under gentle touch|A polished spoon feels even compared with coarse sandpaper. Which property describes the spoon’s surface?|Rough texture;Absorbency;Opacity only|Texture describes the surface rather than the entire object’s shape. Safe touching can compare texture without scratching or damaging samples.|Distinguish surface texture from an object’s overall shape.
Rough texture|A surface with noticeable bumps or irregularities|Sandpaper helps remove small surface layers because of which texture?|Perfectly smooth;Transparent;Stretchy|A rough surface can rub against and abrade another material. The same property can be unsuitable for something meant to feel gentle on skin.|Give one useful and one unsuitable use for a rough surface.
Transparent|Allowing enough light through for objects to be seen clearly|A clear plastic window lets a learner see a picture behind it. Which property is shown?|Opaque;Absorbent;Soft only|Light passes through so the image remains visible. Being colourless and being transparent are related observations but not the same definition.|Explain why transparency is useful for a viewing window.
Opaque|Not allowing objects to be seen through the material because light is blocked|A cardboard cover hides the picture behind it. Which optical property is shown?|Transparent;Soluble;Elastic only|Blocking light protects privacy or shades an object. Thinness alone does not prove that a material will be transparent.|Explain why a thin card may still be opaque.
Flexible|Able to bend without readily breaking under suitable conditions|A plastic folder bends slightly without snapping. Which useful property is shown?|Brittle;Always transparent;Absorbent only|Flexibility concerns bending. A material can be flexible but not stretch very much, so flexibility and elasticity should not be confused.|Distinguish bending from stretching using a familiar example.
Absorbent|Able to take liquid into spaces within a material|A cloth towel takes up spilled water. Which property is useful?|Waterproof;Opaque only;Metallic|Water enters spaces in the fibres. A waterproof surface instead resists water passing through it.|Explain why an absorbent towel is more useful than a waterproof sheet for drying hands.
Waterproof|Resistant to water passing through under the intended conditions|A raincoat keeps rain from soaking clothing underneath. Which property is required?|Absorbent;Soluble;Fragile|The coating or material resists water penetration. Seams and tears can still allow leaks, so the whole object must be checked.|Explain why a raincoat with a tear may leak despite waterproof fabric.
Material selection|Choosing a substance for an object based on the properties needed|A learner chooses absorbent cloth for a towel instead of a plastic sheet. What process is being carried out?|Seed germination;Condensation;Animal reproduction|The intended use determines which properties matter. No single material is best for every purpose.|Explain how the required use should guide a material choice.''')

add(4,6,'Liquid mixtures and simple separation','B4.1.2.2.1','B4.1.2.1.2','The source suffix is absent locally; liquid–liquid mixture description B4.1.2.2.1 is used within the scheduled Materials block.',
'Place small amounts of cooking oil and water in a labelled clear container. Observe before and after gentle stirring, then after standing. Compare with a teacher-prepared safe uniform liquid mixture, such as water with a little food colouring. Use drawings to plan separation without tasting.',
'Use familiar, non-toxic materials only. No fuels, unknown solvents or flames. Do not drink mixtures; clean slippery spills immediately.',
'''Mixture|Two or more substances physically combined|Water and cooking oil are put into the same container. What has been made?|A new pure element;A single animal cell;A seed|The substances remain present even when stirred. Putting materials together does not necessarily form a new chemical substance.|Explain why both original liquids are still present after mixing.
Immiscible liquids|Liquids that do not form one uniform liquid phase when combined|Oil and water form separate layers after standing. How are they described?|Miscible liquids;Solid crystals;Pure gases|The liquids remain in separate phases. Shaking can make small droplets temporarily but does not permanently make them mix uniformly.|Explain why shaking does not permanently remove the two layers.
Miscible liquids|Liquids that mix uniformly under the stated conditions|A teacher’s approved pair of liquids forms one uniform phase after mixing. Which term describes the pair?|Immiscible liquids;Undissolved pebbles;Two solid layers|The pair mixes at the liquid level rather than separating into layers. A uniform appearance alone does not show that only one substance is present.|Explain why a uniform mixture can still contain two substances.
Layer|A distinct region of one liquid above or below another|After standing, oil occupies a separate upper region in water. What is this region called?|Embryo;Root hair;Vapour only|Distinct regions make the boundary visible and can support separation. The location depends partly on density, not simply on colour.|Explain why visible layers are useful when planning separation.
Density|Mass per unit volume of a substance|Equal volumes of cooking oil and water have different masses. Which property differs?|Length only;Colour only;Temperature scale|Cooking oil usually has lower density than water and forms the upper layer. Density is not the same as the total mass of a large container.|Explain why saying “oil floats because there is less oil” is not a good explanation.
Settling time|The period allowed for a disturbed mixture to separate or become less turbulent|A class leaves stirred oil and water untouched before observing the boundary. What are they allowing?|Germination;Combustion;Freezing|Small droplets can combine into clearer layers while the mixture stands. Repeated shaking delays the observation of a stable boundary.|Explain why observations immediately after shaking may differ from observations later.
Decantation|Carefully pouring off one separated liquid region without greatly disturbing the rest|A teacher gently pours an upper layer into another vessel. Which simple method is attempted?|Grinding;Melting;Magnetism|Careful pouring can remove part of a layer but may mix some liquid at the boundary. It is not always a complete separation.|Explain one limitation of separating layers by pouring.
Separating funnel|An apparatus with a tap used to separate suitable immiscible liquid layers|A teacher drains the lower liquid through a tap while watching the boundary. Which apparatus is used?|Thermometer;Balance;Microscope|The lower layer leaves first, and the tap is closed near the boundary. The apparatus is unsuitable for separating two fully miscible liquids by this method.|Explain why a visible boundary matters when using this apparatus.
Observation|A record of what is noticed or measured during an investigation|A pupil writes “two layers formed after five minutes”. What kind of record is this?|An unsupported guess;A safety rule only;A fictional story|The statement records an event and a time rather than claiming an untested cause. Explanations should be based on such evidence.|Separate an observation about a mixture from a possible explanation.
Fair comparison|A comparison keeping relevant conditions the same except the chosen variable|Two groups compare stirring and not stirring using equal liquid volumes and the same waiting time. What design feature helps them?|Changing every condition;Using different liquids;Ignoring measurements|Keeping volume, containers and waiting time similar helps relate the difference to stirring rather than another changed condition.|Name two conditions that should stay the same in this comparison.''')

add(4,7,'Cyclic movements and repeating patterns','B4.2.1.1.1','B4.2.1.1.1','Direct indicator match. Classroom models support the idea of recurrence rather than claiming every cycle has the same duration.',
'Observe or model a rotating wheel, clock hand and swinging pendulum. Record a chosen starting position and when it is reached again. Sort examples into rotating, oscillating and non-repeating movements. Relate regular recurrence to day and night.',
'Use small teacher-controlled models. Keep fingers clear of moving parts; do not run into swinging objects or stare at the Sun.',
'''Cycle|A sequence of events or positions that returns and repeats|A wheel turns until a marked point returns to its starting place, then repeats. What pattern is shown?|A single unrelated event;Permanent rest;Random disappearance|Returning to a starting state allows repetition to be recognised. Different cycles may take very different times.|Explain how a marked point helps demonstrate a repeating pattern.
Rotation|Turning around an axis|A globe spins around its fixed support. Which motion is shown?|Straight-line sliding;Melting;Germination|The object turns about an axis rather than travelling along a straight line. One full turn returns its orientation to the start.|Describe the axis and one full turn in a globe model.
Oscillation|Repeated movement to and fro about a central position|A pendulum moves left, right and back again. What kind of motion is this?|One-way flight;Growth;Condensation|The movement alternates around a central position. One full oscillation includes the return to the same side in the same direction of motion.|Explain why movement from one side to the other is only part of a full cycle.
Period|The time taken for one complete cycle|A learner measures the time for a marked wheel to make one full turn. What quantity is found?|Length;Mass;Density|The measurement is a time interval. Measuring several turns and dividing by their number can reduce the effect of timing errors.|Describe a method for estimating the time of one turn using several turns.
Clock hand|A pointer whose regular turning can show the passage of time|Which familiar object repeatedly turns around a clock face?|A growing leaf;A falling broken cup;A dissolved sugar grain|The hand revisits positions at regular intervals. Different hands complete their cycles at different rates.|Compare the repetition of an hour hand and a minute hand.
Axis|An imaginary or physical line about which an object turns|What does a globe rotate around in a classroom model?|Its shadow only;A growing root;A cloud layer|The axis identifies the line of turning. It should not be confused with a path around another object.|Distinguish an axis of rotation from a path of travel.
Day–night cycle|The regular alternation of daylight and darkness as Earth rotates|A place on Earth repeatedly faces the Sun and then turns away. Which cycle results?|Plant germination;Rusting;Boiling|Earth’s rotation changes whether a place receives direct sunlight. The Sun does not switch off at night.|Explain the role of Earth’s rotation in this repeated pattern.
Orbit|A path followed by an object as it moves around another under gravity|Earth travels around the Sun along what kind of path?|A plant root;A weather thermometer;A liquid layer|Orbiting is movement around another body. Rotation is spinning around the object’s own axis, so the two motions differ.|Distinguish Earth’s orbit from Earth’s rotation.
Prediction|A statement about what is expected to happen based on evidence or a pattern|A learner uses several regular wheel turns to estimate when the next turn will end. What is being made?|A record of yesterday only;A definition of mass;A plant drawing|A repeated pattern supports an expectation that can be tested. A prediction should be checked rather than treated as certain regardless of evidence.|Explain how observing several cycles can support a testable prediction.
Repeated measurement|Measuring the same quantity more than once to check consistency|Three timings are taken for the same model’s turns. What practice improves the evidence?|Ignoring time;Changing the model each time;Recording only a guess|Repeated readings reveal variation and possible mistakes. Conditions should stay similar if results are to be compared.|Explain why one timing may be less reliable than several comparable timings.''')

add(4,8,'Objects in the daytime and nighttime sky','B4.2.1.2.1','B4.2.1.1.2','Source suffix is absent locally; sky-object description B4.2.1.2.1 is used within the scheduled Earth Science block.',
'Use teacher-prepared day and night photographs. Classify Sun, Moon, stars and clouds, noting that the Moon can sometimes be seen by day. Make a dated sky-observation table using safe unaided viewing away from the Sun.',
'Never look directly at the Sun, through binoculars or through a telescope. Children’s observations require adult supervision; do not travel outdoors alone at night.',
'''Sun|The star nearest to Earth, supplying daylight and much of Earth’s energy|Which natural body is the main source of daytime light on Earth?|Moon;Cloud;Streetlight|The Sun produces its own light. Daylight remains dangerous to stare at directly, even when the Sun looks dim through haze.|Explain why the Sun is a light source and why direct viewing is unsafe.
Moon|Earth’s natural satellite, seen mainly because it reflects sunlight|Which body can be visible at night and sometimes during the day without producing its own light like a star?|Sun;Flame;Electric lamp|The Moon reflects sunlight and can be above the horizon in daylight. It is not restricted to appearing only at night.|Explain why the statement “the Moon is only in the night sky” is incorrect.
Star|A very distant luminous object that produces its own light|Tiny points of light in a dark sky are often examples of what?|Cloud droplets;Nearby insects only;Pieces of the Moon|Stars appear small mainly because they are far away. The Sun is a star but appears much larger because it is much nearer.|Explain why the Sun and distant stars can look very different in size.
Cloud|A visible collection of tiny water droplets or ice crystals in the atmosphere|A changing white or grey shape drifts across the daytime sky. What is it likely to be?|A nearby planet;A fixed star;The Sun’s surface|Clouds belong to Earth’s atmosphere and can move with the air. They are not solid objects in outer space.|Explain how a cloud differs from a star.
Planet|A body orbiting a star and seen by reflected light rather than producing starlight|A bright sky object such as Venus reflects sunlight. Which category fits it?|Cloud;Star producing its own light;Artificial lamp|Planets can appear as bright points from Earth. Their appearance alone may not reliably distinguish them without information or a sky guide.|Explain why a bright point in the sky is not always a star.
Atmosphere|The layer of gases surrounding Earth|In which part of Earth’s environment do ordinary weather clouds occur?|Deep inside the Sun;Inside a seed;Below all rocks|Clouds form within the atmosphere, relatively close compared with the Moon or stars. This distinguishes weather phenomena from distant astronomical objects.|Explain why clouds are much closer to us than the Moon.
Horizon|The apparent boundary where the sky meets the ground or sea|What boundary is used when describing a body as rising into view?|The centre of a flower;The bottom of a cup;A root tip|It is an apparent boundary affected by the observer’s location. Buildings or hills may block a clear view of it.|Explain why observers at different locations may have different views near the horizon.
Shadow|A darker region formed when an object blocks light|A tree blocks sunlight and produces a dark patch on the ground. What is formed?|A new Moon;A cloud inside the ground;A star|The blocked light creates the darker region. Changes in light direction alter a shadow’s direction and length.|Explain why a tree’s shadow can change during the day.
Reflected light|Light redirected from a surface rather than newly produced by that surface|Moonlight reaches our eyes mainly as which kind of light?|Light made by lunar flames;Sound waves;Heat from a street drain|Sunlight strikes the Moon and some is directed toward Earth. The Moon therefore acts differently from the Sun as a light source.|Trace the path that makes the Moon visible to us.
Sky record|A dated description or drawing of visible sky conditions and objects|A pupil records time, cloud cover and whether the Moon is visible. What useful record is made?|A food recipe;A soil-mass calculation;A fictional weather claim|Recording time and conditions allows observations to be compared. Failure to see an object may reflect cloud cover rather than its absence from the sky.|Explain why cloud cover should be recorded along with visible sky objects.''')

add(4,9,'Evaporation, transpiration and evapotranspiration','B4.2.1.3.1','B4.2.1.1.3','Source suffix is absent locally; evapotranspiration description B4.2.1.3.1 supplies the scheduled Earth Science lesson.',
'Observe equal small amounts of water in labelled dishes over time. Separately, the teacher places a clear bag briefly over a leafy shoot and an empty control bag in similar conditions. Record droplets and discuss limitations; remove the bag promptly to avoid harming the plant.',
'No heating equipment is required. Do not leave plastic bags where children or animals can place them over their heads. Use only healthy familiar plants and avoid excessive heat.',
'''Evaporation|The change of liquid water at its surface into water vapour|A shallow puddle becomes smaller on a dry afternoon without soaking into the ground. Which process can explain the loss?|Freezing;Germination;Rusting|Water can enter the air as vapour from the surface. It need not reach boiling temperature for this to occur.|Explain why a puddle can dry without boiling.
Water vapour|Water in its gaseous form, normally invisible|What form of water enters the air during evaporation?|Solid ice;Visible sand;Dry salt|The gas is not normally seen directly. A visible mist consists mainly of tiny liquid droplets rather than the invisible gas itself.|Distinguish invisible water vapour from visible mist.
Transpiration|Loss of water vapour from a plant, mainly through leaf openings|A leafy shoot releases water to the air. Which plant process is involved?|Seed dispersal;Pollination;Freezing|Water travels through the plant and is released mainly from leaves. This loss is not the same as rain falling onto the leaf.|Explain how water from roots can eventually reach the air.
Evapotranspiration|The combined transfer of water to the air by evaporation and plant transpiration|A field loses water from soil surfaces and from leaves. What combined term covers both?|Condensation only;Precipitation only;Freezing only|The term joins water loss from non-living surfaces with water loss through plants. Both contribute water vapour to the atmosphere.|Distinguish the two processes included in this term.
Stomata|Small leaf openings involved in gas exchange and water-vapour loss|Through which leaf structures does much transpiration occur?|Petals only;Large woody thorns;Seed coats only|Openings permit gases to move between the leaf and air. Their regulation affects water loss as well as gas exchange.|Explain why leaf openings matter for both gas exchange and water loss.
Condensation|The change of water vapour into liquid droplets on cooling|Droplets appear inside a bag around a leafy shoot. Which change produces the visible drops?|Melting;Burning;Seed formation|Vapour released into the bag can cool and form liquid. The droplets are evidence to interpret with a control, not direct sight of vapour.|Explain why droplets rather than invisible vapour are seen inside the bag.
Control setup|A comparison arrangement lacking the factor being investigated|An empty bag is kept beside a bag with leaves under similar conditions. What role does the empty bag serve?|A second plant specimen;A fertilizer;A result to ignore|It helps reveal whether droplets could arise without the leaves. Comparing both bags supports a more careful explanation.|Explain why the empty bag improves the plant-water investigation.
Surface area|The extent of an exposed surface|The same volume of water dries faster in a wide shallow dish than a narrow cup under similar conditions. Which feature is greater in the dish?|Water’s chemical identity;Amount of salt necessarily;Air’s mass necessarily|More exposed water surface allows evaporation from a larger area. Other conditions must be comparable for a fair test.|Explain why equal starting volumes are needed when comparing the containers.
Air movement|Movement of surrounding air that can carry water vapour away|A wet cloth often dries faster in a breeze than in still air. Which factor helps explain it?|Absence of all water;A change into rock;No evaporation|Moving air removes moist air near the surface, helping more water evaporate. Temperature and humidity also affect drying.|Explain why a breeze can speed drying without heating water to boiling.
Measurement table|An organised record of values such as time and remaining water|A learner records water level every hour using the same method. What helps organise the evidence?|A guess written only at the end;A drawing without labels;An unrelated shopping list|A table preserves the sequence and units for comparison. Consistent measuring methods make differences more meaningful.|Name two headings needed for a useful water-loss record.''')

add(4,10,'Making water safer and preventing recontamination','B4.2.1.5.1','B4.2.1.1.4','Source suffix is absent locally; safe-water description B4.2.1.5.1 supplies this Earth Science lesson. This is education, not a guarantee that a classroom treatment produces potable water.',
'Use drawings to order settling, appropriate filtration, approved disinfection and clean storage. Demonstrate only removal of visible particles using a teacher-prepared model, clearly labelled NOT FOR DRINKING. Compare pictures of safe and unsafe storage practices.',
'Do not drink demonstration water. Heating and any disinfectant handling are adult-only. Follow local public-health guidance and product instructions; no chemical doses are supplied here.',
'''Potable water|Water considered suitable for drinking under applicable safety guidance|Which term describes water suitable for drinking, not merely water that looks clear?|Muddy water;Salt water automatically;Any transparent liquid|Safety includes more than appearance. Harmful microbes or chemicals can be invisible, so a trusted safe supply or approved treatment is needed.|Explain why clear water is not automatically suitable for drinking.
Contamination|Introduction of harmful or unwanted substances into water|Dirty hands touch the inside of a drinking-water container. What risk is introduced?|Purification;Photosynthesis;Germination|Microbes or dirt can enter a previously safe supply. Preventing contamination after treatment is as important as the treatment step.|Explain how treated water can become unsafe again.
Sedimentation|Allowing suspended particles to settle under gravity|Muddy water is left still so heavier particles collect at the bottom. Which process occurs?|Boiling;Melting;Transpiration|Settling can remove some visible particles from the upper water but does not reliably remove dissolved chemicals or all microbes.|State one benefit and one limitation of allowing muddy water to stand.
Filtration|Passing a mixture through a barrier that retains selected particles|A classroom filter catches visible sand while water passes through. Which method is demonstrated?|Evaporation;Burning;Pollination|Simple filters may remove visible particles without removing pathogens or dissolved pollutants. Demonstration filtrate must not be treated as safe drinking water.|Explain why filtered classroom water must not be drunk.
Boiling|Heating water until it boils, used under suitable guidance to inactivate many pathogens|Which heat-based method can help disinfect microbiologically unsafe water when properly carried out by an adult?|Stirring with a dirty stick;Adding sand;Leaving it uncovered|Proper boiling addresses many microbes, but it does not reliably remove harmful dissolved chemicals. Cooling and storage must avoid recontamination.|Explain one limitation of boiling as a water-safety method.
Disinfection|A treatment intended to inactivate harmful microorganisms|An approved treatment is used according to official instructions to reduce disease-causing microbes. What is its purpose called?|Adding dirt;Making water colourful;Increasing sediment|The method must suit the contamination and be correctly applied. A disinfectant is not a universal remover of all chemical pollutants.|Explain why treatment choice must depend on the type of contamination.
Covered storage|Keeping water in a clean container protected by an appropriate lid|A clean drinking-water vessel is closed after filling. Which protection practice is used?|Open exposure;Waste dumping;Chemical mixing by pupils|A lid limits entry of dirt, insects and hands, but the container itself must also be clean. A dirty lid or vessel can still contaminate water.|Explain why both a clean container and a cover matter.
Clean dispensing|Removing water without dipping dirty hands or utensils into the supply|A household pours water or uses a clean tap rather than reaching into the vessel. Which practice reduces contamination?|Open dumping;Soil mixing;Using unwashed cups inside storage|Avoiding contact with the stored water reduces transfer of microbes. A clean serving cup still matters after dispensing.|Explain how dipping a dirty cup can affect the entire stored supply.
Protected source|A water source managed to reduce entry of waste and pollutants|A community prevents waste dumping near its water supply. What type of protection is strengthened?|A source left open to rubbish;An evaporation experiment;A seedbed|Prevention reduces contamination before collection. It does not remove the need for monitoring or correct treatment when required.|Explain why prevention near a source can help the whole community.
Hygiene|Practices that reduce transfer of dirt and disease-causing organisms|Hands are washed before handling clean water containers. Which broad practice is demonstrated?|Rusting;Weather forecasting;Seed dispersal|Clean hands and utensils reduce contamination during collection, storage and serving. Multiple barriers work together rather than relying on one step alone.|Name two hygiene practices that protect drinking water.''')

add(4,11,'Plant parts and their functions','B4.2.2.1.1','B4.2.2.1.1','Direct indicator match; function vocabulary supports observation and labelling.',
'Observe a familiar plant or a clear labelled drawing. Trace water from soil through roots and stem to leaves. Match root, stem, leaf, flower, fruit and seed to functions, and produce a labelled diagram with arrows that end on the correct part.',
'Do not taste plant parts or damage protected plants. Avoid thorns and irritant sap; wash hands after soil contact.',
'''Root|A plant part commonly anchoring the plant and absorbing water and dissolved minerals|Which part usually takes water from the soil into a plant?|Petal;Fruit skin;Pollen grain|Roots hold the plant and absorb water and minerals. They do not normally absorb prepared sugary food directly from soil.|State two important functions of roots.
Stem|A supporting plant part containing transport pathways between roots and leaves|Which part holds leaves up and links them with the roots?|Seed coat only;Pollen only;Fruit pulp only|Support positions leaves for light, and internal pathways transport materials. The stem is not merely a solid stick with no internal functions.|Explain how support and transport both help the plant.
Leaf|A plant organ commonly specialised for making food in light|Which green part is usually a major site of photosynthesis?|Root hair;Seed coat;Flower stalk only|Many leaves receive light and exchange gases. Green tissues use light energy to make sugars from water and carbon dioxide.|Explain why leaves are often positioned where they can receive light.
Flower|A reproductive structure of a flowering plant|Which part may produce pollen and later give rise to seeds after reproductive processes?|Root hair;Tree bark only;Absorbing root tip|Flowers support reproduction, not just decoration. After pollination and fertilisation, seeds may develop and parts of the flower may form fruit.|Explain why a flower is more than a colourful decoration.
Fruit|A structure commonly developing from a flower’s ovary and containing or protecting seeds|A mango surrounds a seed with fleshy tissue. Which structure is the mango as a whole?|Root hair;Leaf blade;Stem node only|Fruits protect seeds and can help dispersal. Not all fruits are sweet or eaten by people.|State two ways a fruit can help the next generation of plants.
Seed|A structure containing a young plant and resources or tissues supporting its early development|What can grow into a new plant when suitable germination conditions are present?|A clean stone;A glass bead;A plastic button|A viable seed contains a living embryo. Water, oxygen and suitable temperature support germination, but a damaged or dead seed may not grow.|Explain why not every seed necessarily germinates.
Seed coat|The outer protective covering of a seed|Which part protects the young plant inside a bean seed?|Petal;Leaf stalk;Pollen tube|The covering provides protection. Water uptake and growth lead to changes that allow the emerging root to break through it.|Explain why a protective covering must still permit germination under suitable conditions.
Photosynthesis|The process in which green tissues use light to make sugars from water and carbon dioxide|A green leaf uses sunlight to make food. Which process is this?|Digestion in a stomach;Rusting;Sedimentation|Light supplies energy; water and carbon dioxide supply materials. The plant is not eating soil in the way an animal eats food.|Distinguish the source of energy from the materials used to make plant food.
Pollination|Transfer of pollen to a flower’s stigma|An insect carries pollen from one flower to a suitable stigma. Which process occurs?|Germination;Root absorption;Evaporation|Pollen transfer is part of reproduction. It is not identical to fertilisation, which is a later biological event when successful reproduction proceeds.|Explain why pollination and seed germination are different processes.
Transport|Movement of water and other materials between plant parts|Water taken up by roots reaches leaves through internal pathways. Which function is shown?|Only reproduction;Only seed protection;Only shading|Materials move between organs that perform different jobs. Damage to important transport pathways can affect distant parts of a plant.|Explain why harm to a stem can affect leaves above it.''')

add(4,12,'Seeds and germination','B4.2.2.1.2','B4.2.2.1.2','Direct indicator match. Germination conditions are distinguished from the later needs of an established green seedling.',
'Place viable bean seeds on moist paper in a labelled transparent container with access to air. Compare with a dry-paper control under similar warmth. Record daily changes, sketch the first root and shoot, and stop if mould develops. Keep a simple time-and-observation table.',
'Do not eat experimental seeds. Avoid mould contact and overwatering; wash hands after handling materials. Use clean containers and teacher supervision.',
'''Germination|The beginning of growth of a seed’s embryo under suitable conditions|A bean seed swells, splits its coat and produces a young root. What process is beginning?|Pollination;Rusting;Condensation|A viable embryo resumes growth. This is different from producing the seed inside a flower and fruit.|Distinguish germination from the earlier production of a seed.
Embryo|The young plant inside a seed|Which living structure develops into the seedling?|Seed jar;Soil pebble;Plastic label|The embryo contains the beginnings of root and shoot. A viable embryo, not simply a seed-shaped object, is needed for growth.|Explain why a glass bead cannot germinate even if it receives water.
Seed coat|The protective outer covering of a seed|Which covering often splits when a soaked viable bean begins to grow?|Petal;Stem bark;Leaf vein|The covering protects the contents, but swelling and growing tissues can break through it. Splitting alone without continued growth is not complete evidence of healthy germination.|Explain why continued observations are needed after a seed coat splits.
Radicle|The young root that commonly emerges first during germination|A small root appears from the seed before the shoot is visible. What is it called?|Petal;Fruit;Pollen grain|The emerging root helps establish absorption and anchorage. Its function differs from the shoot that later carries leaves.|Explain why early root growth helps a new seedling.
Plumule|The young shoot of the embryo|Which developing structure grows upward and gives rise to shoot tissues and leaves?|Root hair only;Seed jar;Soil grain|The shoot supports future leaves and access to light. Roots and shoots develop different structures suited to different jobs.|Compare the main roles of the young root and young shoot.
Cotyledon|A seed leaf that may store food or help transfer stored food to the embryo|The fleshy seed leaves of a bean supply food during early growth. What are they called?|Petals;Root hairs;Cloud droplets|Stored food supports growth before the seedling makes enough food by photosynthesis. Cotyledon roles vary among different seeds.|Explain why a germinating seed can begin growing before it has large green leaves.
Moisture|Water available to the seed without completely excluding air|Moist paper supports germination better than dry paper for otherwise similar viable beans. Which need is supplied?|Paint;Petrol;Dry sand only|Water uptake activates growth processes. Excess water can exclude oxygen, so damp conditions are not the same as submerging every seed.|Explain why both dryness and excessive water can prevent healthy germination.
Oxygen|A gas needed for respiration by a germinating seed|Which gas must be available so a germinating seed can release energy from stored food?|Helium;Smoke only;Carbon monoxide|Respiration supplies energy for growth. Waterlogged conditions may reduce gas availability even when plenty of water is present.|Explain why a seed needs air as well as water.
Suitable temperature|A range of warmth allowing germination processes to proceed normally|Two moist seeds grow differently in very cold and moderately warm conditions. Which environmental factor differs?|The colour of the label;The number of jar sides;The name of the seed|Growth processes depend on temperature; extremely hot or cold conditions may prevent germination. The suitable range depends on the species.|Explain why “the hotter the better” is not a sound germination rule.
Control|A comparison setup used to help test the role of one factor|Dry paper is compared with moist paper while seed type and warmth are kept the same. What is the dry setup’s role?|A guaranteed best treatment;A food supply;A plant flower|The comparison helps isolate the effect of moisture. Several seeds in each condition reduce the risk of drawing conclusions from one unusual seed.|Explain why the same seed type and several seeds should be used in both setups.''')

add(5,2,'Living, dead and never-alive things','B5.1.1.2.1','B5.1.1.2.1','Direct indicator match; dormant seeds are distinguished from dead material.',
'Classify photographs of a growing plant, sleeping animal, viable dry seed, dead leaf, wooden chair, rock and plastic bottle. Give evidence for each choice. Discuss why movement alone and present appearance alone can be misleading.',
'Do not bring decaying animals or mouldy material into class. Use clean samples or photographs and wash hands after handling natural materials.',
'''Living thing|An organism carrying out life processes, even if some are not obvious at the moment|A healthy seedling grows and responds to light. Which category fits it?|Dead organism;Never-alive mineral;Plastic object|Growth and response support the classification, while respiration also occurs. Several life-process observations provide stronger evidence than one superficial feature.|Give two pieces of evidence that a healthy seedling is alive.
Dead organism|An organism that once lived but has irreversibly stopped its life processes|A formerly living animal has died and no longer carries out life processes. Which category applies?|Healthy dormant seed;Never-alive stone;Growing seedling|Its history includes life, unlike a rock. Temporary stillness or sleep does not by itself show death.|Explain why a sleeping animal should not be classified this way merely because it is still.
Never-alive material|A material that has never itself been a living organism|Which category fits a mineral crystal considered as an object rather than its surroundings?|Living animal;Dead plant;Dormant embryo|A crystal can become larger by deposition without carrying out the integrated life processes of an organism. Growth-like change alone is insufficient.|Explain why a growing crystal is not treated as a living plant.
Dormancy|A low-activity state in which a viable organism can resume growth under suitable conditions|A viable dry seed later germinates after receiving suitable conditions. What earlier state explains its inactivity?|Death;Melting;Permanent mineral growth|The embryo remained alive but relatively inactive. A failure to grow immediately does not prove that the seed is dead.|Explain why a dry seed needs a viability test rather than classification by appearance alone.
Dead plant material|Material derived from a plant that is no longer a living organism|A dry wooden plank was once part of a living tree. Which description fits its origin and present status?|A living tree;Never-derived-from-life material;A growing embryo|The plank has a biological origin but does not carry out the life processes of a whole tree. A manufactured object can contain once-living material.|Explain why a wooden chair is not alive even though its material came from a tree.
Artificial movement|Motion produced by a mechanism rather than evidence of an organism’s life processes|A battery-powered toy car moves across the floor. What best describes this movement?|Proof of animal life;Plant growth;Seed respiration|A motor moves the toy. It does not thereby reproduce biologically, obtain nutrients as an organism or grow through cellular processes.|Explain why movement alone is an unreliable test for life.
Respiration|Cellular processes releasing usable energy from food|Which continuing process supports a resting living animal even when it is not walking?|Rusting;Crystal deposition;Combustion in a toy motor|Living cells require energy for maintenance. Respiration is chemical activity in cells, not merely visible movement or breathing motions.|Explain why a still living organism may remain biologically active.
Response to stimuli|A change in activity caused by detecting an environmental change|A plant shoot bends gradually toward light. Which life characteristic is shown?|Permanent death;Plastic manufacture;Melting only|The plant responds through differential growth rather than walking. A slow response still provides evidence relevant to life.|Explain how a plant can respond without moving from place to place.
Evidence|Observations or measurements used to support a conclusion|A learner uses growth records and a successful germination test to justify a classification. What supports the claim?|A guess without testing;An unrelated preference;The colour of the container alone|Recorded observations support the explanation. Where evidence is incomplete, the learner should acknowledge uncertainty rather than invent a result.|Explain why a classification should include reasons rather than a label alone.
Biological reproduction|Production of new organisms by living systems|A bean plant produces viable seeds capable of making new bean plants. Which life process is represented?|A photocopier copying paper;A crystal cracking;A toy moving|New organisms arise through biological processes. A machine producing similar-looking objects is not evidence that the machine is alive.|Compare biological reproduction with a photocopier producing copies.''')

# The material-property concepts are shared; B5 has a direct curriculum reference.
m=copy.deepcopy(LESSONS[(4,5)])
m.update(grade=5,week=3,page=26,title='Classifying materials and justifying their uses',codes='B5.1.2.1.1',source_codes='B5.1.2.1.1',mapping_note='Direct indicator match. Core property explanations and practice are shared with B4’s introductory editorial unit; B5 activity demands justified comparisons.',activity='Compare paper, cloth, wood, metal and plastic using a property matrix. Test absorption and flexibility safely, keeping sample sizes and test conditions comparable. Recommend materials for a towel, window, raincoat and folder, giving both an advantage and a limitation for each choice.')
LESSONS[(5,3)]=m

add(5,4,'Reversible and irreversible changes','B5.1.2.2.1','B5.1.2.2.1','Direct indicator match. Reversibility is discussed under ordinary classroom conditions; not every irreversible change is chemical.',
'Observe melting ice and refreezing through photographs or teacher-prepared samples. Compare dissolving salt, tearing paper, cooking an egg and burning wood using cards. Explain which original material or shape could be recovered and by what safe method.',
'Learners do not burn materials or handle hot equipment. Heating demonstrations are teacher-only; no tasting experimental mixtures.',
'''Reversible change|A change that can be undone to recover the starting material or condition by an appropriate process|Ice melts and the water can later be frozen again. Which broad description fits?|Permanent disappearance;Biological reproduction;An always-chemical change|Changing temperature can return water to solid form. The water remains the same substance despite its state change.|Explain how melting and freezing can form a reversible pair.
Irreversible change|A change not readily undone to restore the original under ordinary conditions|A cooked egg cannot simply be cooled back into a raw egg. Which description is appropriate?|Simple reversible melting;Condensation only;Temporary bending|Cooking changes the egg’s materials in ways not reversed by cooling. The conclusion concerns ordinary practical reversal, not merely a change in temperature.|Explain why cooling does not undo cooking an egg.
Melting|A change from solid to liquid|An ice cube becomes liquid water in a warm room. Which change occurs?|Freezing;Condensation;Seed germination|Energy transfer changes the arrangement and movement of particles. Water is not lost merely because its solid shape disappears.|Explain why melting ice does not mean the water has ceased to exist.
Freezing|A change from liquid to solid|Water placed in a suitable freezer becomes ice. Which process occurs?|Boiling;Rusting;Dissolving|Removing enough thermal energy permits solid formation. It is the reverse state change of melting.|Describe how freezing can reverse an earlier melting change.
Evaporation|Change of liquid to gas from a surface below or at boiling conditions|Water slowly leaves a salt solution exposed to dry air. Which process removes the water?|Rusting;Freezing;Germination|Water enters the air as vapour while dissolved salt may remain. Evaporation is not the same as destroying the water substance.|Explain how evaporation can help recover salt from a solution.
Condensation|Change of a gas or vapour into liquid|Water droplets form when water vapour cools on a suitable surface. Which change occurs?|Melting;Combustion;Tearing|Cooling can return vapour to liquid. The water in the droplets was previously in gaseous form.|Explain how this process relates to evaporation.
Dissolving|Formation of a solution as a solute becomes distributed in a solvent|Salt grains become invisible when stirred into water. Which process occurs?|Burning;Freezing;Growth|The salt remains distributed through the solution and can be recovered by suitable water removal. It has not disappeared from existence.|Explain why invisible dissolved salt can still be present.
Combustion|A burning reaction that commonly produces new substances and releases energy|Wood burns to ash and gases. Which process is shown?|Freezing;Melting only;Simple stretching|Ash and gases differ from the starting wood. Cooling the products does not rebuild the original wood.|Explain why burning wood is not reversed simply by cooling the ash.
Rusting|A corrosion process involving iron, oxygen and suitable moisture|An iron object slowly develops rust in damp conditions. Which change occurs?|Melting;Condensation;Seed reproduction|Rust is a new corrosion product rather than a temporary change in shape. Preventing exposure can reduce further damage, but drying does not restore lost iron automatically.|Explain why drying a rusty nail does not make it new again.
Physical change|A change such as shape or state without necessarily creating a new substance|Paper is cut into small pieces while remaining paper. Which general kind of change is shown?|Automatic formation of a new element;Biological growth;Photosynthesis|Cutting changes size and shape without making the material a new substance. It may still be difficult to restore the original sheet, showing that reversibility and chemical identity are different questions.|Explain why an irreversible-looking change need not always be chemical.''')

add(5,5,'Forming and separating mixtures','B5.1.2.3.1','B5.1.2.3.1','Direct indicator match; separation is selected according to component properties.',
'Prepare labelled sand-and-water, salt-and-water, and oil-and-water samples. Predict which method suits each. Demonstrate settling and filtration of sand; use diagrams for evaporation and separating-funnel procedures. Record which component is retained or collected.',
'No tasting mixtures. Learners do not heat samples or handle unknown solvents. Demonstration filtrate is not drinking water; clean oil spills promptly.',
'''Mixture|Two or more substances physically combined while retaining their chemical identities|Sand and water are placed together without forming a new pure compound. What is made?|An element;A single atom;A seed|Components can often be separated by physical properties. Their proportions can vary without becoming one fixed-composition pure substance.|Explain why sand and water are treated as a mixture.
Soluble|Able to dissolve in a stated solvent under given conditions|Salt forms a solution in water. Which property of salt in water is demonstrated?|Insoluble in all liquids;Magnetic necessarily;Waterproof necessarily|Solubility depends on both solute and solvent and on conditions. The dissolved substance is still present even when individual grains are unseen.|Explain why the solvent must be named when discussing solubility.
Insoluble|Not dissolving appreciably in a stated solvent under the conditions|Sand remains as grains after stirring in water. Which description applies?|Fully dissolved;Gaseous;A new element|The visible solid can settle or be trapped by a suitable filter. Its behaviour differs from dissolved salt in the same liquid.|Compare the separation of sand from water with removal of dissolved salt.
Filtration|Separation using a porous barrier that retains suitable solid particles|Sand is trapped on filter paper while water passes through. Which method is used?|Distillation only;Melting;Combustion|The solid particle size allows retention, while liquid passes. Dissolved salt would generally pass through ordinary filter paper.|Explain why the same paper cannot remove all dissolved material.
Residue|Material retained on a filter during filtration|What is the sand left on the filter paper called?|Filtrate;Vapour;Solvent gas|It is the retained portion, not the liquid collected below. Naming both portions makes the method and result clear.|Distinguish the retained sand from the collected liquid.
Filtrate|The fluid that passes through a filter|What is the liquid collected below the filter paper called?|Residue;Filter cone;Undissolved gravel|Passing through a filter does not prove drinking-water safety. Dissolved pollutants and some microbes may remain.|Explain why this liquid should not automatically be drunk.
Evaporation|Conversion of liquid to vapour, usable for recovering a non-volatile dissolved solid|Which process can leave salt behind as water from a salt solution enters the air?|Magnetism;Freezing the salt only;Paper cutting|The solvent is removed, leaving the salt. This simple method does not collect the water unless vapour is also captured and condensed.|Explain which component is recovered and which is not collected in an open evaporation setup.
Decantation|Careful pouring off of a liquid from settled material or a suitable separate layer|Clearer liquid is gently poured away from settled sand. Which method is attempted?|Grinding;Melting;Germination|The method works best after settling and with careful pouring. Fine suspended material can still remain in the poured liquid.|Explain one limitation of this method for muddy water.
Immiscible|Describing liquids that form separate phases instead of mixing uniformly|Cooking oil and water form layers after standing. Which description fits them?|Fully miscible;Both solid;Both gaseous|A visible boundary enables separation of suitable liquid layers. Shaking may disperse droplets temporarily without making a permanent solution.|Explain why stirring alone cannot permanently make the two liquids uniform.
Separating funnel|A tapped apparatus used to drain one suitable immiscible liquid layer from another|A teacher drains the lower layer and closes a tap near the oil–water boundary. Which apparatus is used?|Measuring balance;Microscope;Thermometer|The lower layer exits first, so the boundary is watched carefully. Fully miscible liquids do not provide that separable boundary.|Explain why this apparatus is unsuitable for separating dissolved salt from water by draining layers.''')

add(5,6,'How day and night are formed','B5.2.1.1.1','B5.2.1.1.1','Direct indicator match; model limitations are explicitly taught.',
'Use a globe or ball and a steady torch in a dim room. Mark a Ghana location. Rotate the globe while keeping the lamp in one place; observe illumination of the mark. Draw the lit and unlit sides and explain model limitations.',
'Use a battery torch, not flame. Never look at the Sun or direct a bright torch into anyone’s eyes. Keep the room safe to move in.',
'''Rotation|Earth’s turning around its own axis|Which motion chiefly causes the regular alternation of day and night?|Earth’s yearly orbit alone;The Moon blocking all sunlight daily;The Sun switching off|Turning changes which side faces sunlight. The yearly journey around the Sun is a different motion.|Distinguish the motion causing day and night from Earth’s yearly motion.
Axis|The imaginary line about which Earth rotates|What does the globe turn around in a day-and-night model?|Its nearest cloud;A river;The Moon’s shadow only|The line runs between the poles in the model. It is not a physical stick running through real Earth for people to see.|Explain what the globe’s support represents and what it does not literally show.
Daylight|The condition at a place receiving direct sunlight on Earth’s lit side|The Ghana mark on a globe faces the torch. Which condition is represented?|Night;A lunar eclipse necessarily;The absence of the Sun|The lamp-facing side receives light. A full classroom room may still have stray light, so the model simplifies real conditions.|Explain the meaning of the illuminated mark in the model.
Night|The condition at a place turned away from direct sunlight|The Ghana mark turns to the side away from the torch. What is represented?|The Sun burning out;Earth disappearing;Permanent winter|Earth blocks direct sunlight from reaching that side. The Sun continues producing light even though the location faces away.|Explain why night does not mean the Sun has switched off.
Approximately 24 hours|The approximate duration of an ordinary solar day|About how long is the familiar daylight-to-daylight daily cycle?|One month;One year;One minute|The ordinary day is about 24 hours. The classroom model is sped up and is not a time-scale reproduction of Earth’s rotation.|Explain why rotating a model in seconds does not change the real duration of a day.
West to east|The direction of Earth’s rotation when described geographically|In which direction does Earth generally rotate?|East to west;North straight into the Sun;No direction at all|This rotation makes the Sun appear to move across the sky in the opposite broad direction. Appearance from the ground differs from the underlying cause.|Relate Earth’s turning to the Sun’s apparent daily movement.
Sunrise|The apparent appearance of the Sun above the local horizon as Earth turns|A location rotates into the illuminated side and the Sun comes into view. What event is modelled?|The Sun being newly created;A monthly orbit;Plant germination|The observer’s changing orientation brings the Sun into view. Local land and cloud conditions can affect what is actually seen.|Explain why sunrise does not mean the Sun has just begun to exist.
Sunset|The apparent disappearance of the Sun below the local horizon as Earth turns|A location rotates away from the illuminated side and the Sun leaves view. What event is modelled?|Earth stopping permanently;The Sun becoming a planet;Seed dispersal|The observer’s view changes because Earth turns. The Sun continues lighting other parts of Earth.|Explain how another place can still have daylight when your location has sunset.
Globe model|A simplified representation of Earth used to investigate its shape and movements|A ball with a marked location is used beside a lamp. What kind of teaching aid is this?|An exact full-size Earth;A real star;A natural satellite|The model helps explain relationships but is not to scale in distance, size or speed. Model limits should be stated rather than hidden.|State two ways this classroom model differs from the real Earth–Sun system.
Simultaneous day and night|The occurrence of daylight in one part of Earth while another faces away from the Sun|The lamp lights one hemisphere while the opposite side is dark. Which global condition is demonstrated?|Day everywhere at once;Night everywhere at once;The Sun travelling through Earth|Only the side facing the source receives direct light. Locations on opposite sides can therefore experience different times of day.|Explain why all places on Earth do not have daylight at the same time.''')

add(5,7,'Benefits of the Sun to Earth','B5.2.1.2.1','B5.2.1.2.1','Direct indicator match; solar benefits are taught alongside safe exposure.',
'Create an energy-connection chart linking the Sun to green plants, food chains, warming, drying and solar devices. Compare safe shaded and sunlit surfaces by observation without touching hot objects. Discuss both benefits and risks of excessive exposure.',
'Never look directly at the Sun. Avoid prolonged heat exposure; use shade, suitable clothing and normal school sun-safety arrangements. Do not use lenses to focus sunlight.',
'''Solar energy|Energy received from the Sun as radiation|What is the original energy source driving many natural processes on Earth?|Energy made by the Moon’s light production;A classroom battery only;Deep-sea salt only|Solar radiation supplies light and contributes warming. It supports many processes but is not the only possible energy source anywhere on Earth.|Give two different processes supported by energy from the Sun.
Photosynthesis|Use of light energy by green organisms to make sugars from water and carbon dioxide|Which process links sunlight to food production in a green leaf?|Rusting;Digestion in an animal;Sedimentation|Light supplies energy for making food. Soil minerals and water support growth but are not substitutes for the light-driven process.|Explain why sunlight is important to many green plants.
Food chain|A sequence showing transfer of food matter and energy between organisms|Grass uses sunlight, a grasshopper eats grass and a bird eats the grasshopper. What relationship is represented?|A water-pipe map;A rock scale;A clock cycle|Energy stored by the producer passes through feeding relationships. Organisms do not pass all received energy unchanged to the next level.|Explain how a bird’s food energy can be linked indirectly to sunlight.
Warming|An increase in temperature as energy is transferred to a body|A surface exposed to sunlight becomes warmer than a similar shaded surface. Which effect is shown?|Automatic freezing;Loss of all energy;Creation of a new element|Absorbed radiation can increase temperature. Colour, material, wind and exposure time also affect the result.|Name two conditions to control when comparing warming in sun and shade.
Evaporation|Surface loss of liquid water into the air as vapour|Sunlight helps wet clothes dry by supporting which process?|Seed formation;Rusting only;Freezing|Energy helps water enter the air. Wind and humidity also influence drying, so sunlight is not the only relevant factor.|Explain why drying rates can differ even on two sunny days.
Water cycle|Repeated movement of water among atmosphere, land and water bodies|The Sun helps water leave the sea, later forming clouds and rain. Which larger cycle is supported?|A stone’s growth cycle;A plastic-making cycle;A daily toy-car race|Solar energy supports evaporation and the return of water to the atmosphere. Gravity and other conditions help later movements such as rainfall and runoff.|Explain the Sun’s role without claiming it causes every water-cycle step in the same way.
Solar panel|A device designed to convert incoming sunlight into usable energy, with photovoltaic panels producing electricity|Which device can power a suitable lamp using photovoltaic conversion?|A dry stone alone;A paper label;An unconnected spoon|Photovoltaic cells convert light to electricity. Available power depends on sunlight and the system, while storage can support use at other times.|Explain why a solar-powered system may include a battery.
Solar heating|Using absorbed sunlight to raise the temperature of a material such as water|A designed solar water heater warms water without burning fuel at the point of heating. Which use is shown?|Photosynthesis inside metal;Freezing by sunlight;Animal respiration|Incoming radiation is absorbed and transferred as thermal energy. A hot-water device is not necessarily producing electricity.|Distinguish solar water heating from photovoltaic electricity generation.
Visible light|The part of radiation that human eyes can detect under suitable conditions|Which contribution from the Sun allows normal outdoor vision by day?|Sound from the Sun;Solid particles only;Underground minerals|Light reaches objects and some is reflected to the eyes. Visibility is different from staring directly at the source, which is unsafe for the Sun.|Explain how sunlight helps us see objects without looking at the Sun.
Sun protection|Practices reducing harmful excessive sunlight or heat exposure|A class uses shade and suitable clothing during an outdoor activity. Which practice is shown?|Blocking all plant photosynthesis;Removing the atmosphere;Increasing unsafe exposure|Sunlight has benefits but excessive ultraviolet exposure and heat can harm people. Protection allows safer use of outdoor environments.|Give two sensible measures for a school activity in strong sunshine.''')

add(5,8,'Evaporation, condensation and cloud formation','B5.2.1.3.1–2','B5.2.1.3.1–2','Direct indicator match; clouds are tiny droplets or ice crystals, not simply invisible water vapour.',
'Use a cold sealed container to observe droplets forming on its outside; keep a similar uncooled container as a comparison. Build an arrow diagram of evaporation, condensation, cloud formation, precipitation and collection. Label water’s state at each step.',
'Use cold or room-temperature materials rather than learner-operated boiling apparatus. Wipe spills and do not drink experiment water.',
'''Evaporation|Movement of liquid water into the air as water vapour from a surface|Water in a shallow tray gradually decreases under suitable drying conditions. Which process may explain it?|Freezing;Crystal growth only;Seed reproduction|Surface molecules enter the air, and this can occur below boiling temperature. Control leakage and absorption before attributing all loss to evaporation.|Explain one alternative cause of falling water level that a fair investigation should exclude.
Water vapour|Water in gaseous form, normally invisible|Which form of water is present in humid air even when no cloud is visible?|Only ice cubes;Visible sand;Solid salt|The gas is usually invisible. Visible mist and clouds contain small droplets or ice rather than being a direct view of the invisible gas.|Distinguish humid air from a visible cloud.
Condensation|Conversion of water vapour into liquid droplets|Drops form on the outside of a cold sealed bottle. Which process explains them?|Liquid leaking through intact glass necessarily;Combustion;Germination|Moist air cools near the surface and water condenses. With a sound sealed container, the drops need not have passed through its wall.|Explain the likely source of water on a cold sealed bottle’s outside.
Cooling|A decrease in temperature as energy is transferred away|Moist air rises and its temperature decreases. Which change can promote cloud formation?|Unlimited heating;Turning all air into rock;Creating new water atoms|Cooling can bring air to conditions where vapour condenses on suitable particles. Merely rising does not guarantee a cloud without sufficient moisture and suitable conditions.|Explain why cooling and moisture both matter in cloud formation.
Cloud droplets|Tiny suspended liquid drops forming part of many clouds|What makes many clouds visible rather than being entirely invisible vapour?|Only dry oxygen gas;Solid sand grains alone;Empty space|The droplets scatter light and may remain suspended in moving air. A cloud may also contain ice crystals, especially at colder temperatures.|Explain why calling a cloud “only water gas” is inaccurate.
Condensation nuclei|Small airborne particles on which water can begin condensing|Tiny particles help cloud droplets start forming in moist cooling air. What role do these particles play?|They are all raindrops already;They replace water entirely;They are roots|Water condenses onto small surfaces. This does not mean every visible cloud is simply dust without water.|Explain the difference between a supporting particle and the water droplet around it.
Precipitation|Water falling from clouds to Earth, such as rain|Cloud droplets grow sufficiently and fall as rain. Which water-cycle stage is this?|Evaporation;Plant absorption only;Underground storage only|Droplets or ice particles can grow until falling becomes important. Not every cloud produces rain at the observer’s location.|Explain why the presence of clouds does not guarantee immediate rain.
Runoff|Water flowing over the land surface toward lower areas|Rainwater moves along the ground into a stream. Which process is shown?|Root respiration;Atmospheric condensation;Solar reflection|Water follows slopes and channels. Some rainfall also soaks into the ground, so not all rain becomes surface flow.|Distinguish water flowing over the ground from water entering soil.
Infiltration|Entry of water from the surface into soil|Rainwater soaks into a garden rather than immediately running away. Which process occurs?|Transpiration from leaves;Cloud formation;Freezing above the Sun|Soil spaces receive water that may later be stored or move deeper. Surface condition and soil properties affect the rate.|Explain why paved ground and a garden may respond differently to rainfall.
Collection|Accumulation of water in places such as rivers, lakes and oceans|After runoff, water gathers in a reservoir. Which broad water-cycle stage is represented?|Combustion;Pollination;Seed dormancy|Collected water can later evaporate or be used and returned through other routes. A cycle has connected pathways rather than a single permanent end point.|Explain why collection is not the final stopping point for all water.''')

add(5,9,'Carbon dioxide: uses and effects','B5.2.1.4.1','B5.2.1.4.1','Direct indicator match; distinguish carbon dioxide from carbon monoxide and avoid unsafe gas demonstrations.',
'Use a process chart linking respiration, combustion and photosynthesis. Sort statements about useful roles, greenhouse effects and unsafe high concentrations. Interpret simple pictures of plant growth, a fire extinguisher label and poor ventilation without releasing gases.',
'No gas production, sealed-space breathing tests or fire-extinguisher discharge by learners. Never enter confined spaces or use burning fuel indoors for demonstrations.',
'''Carbon dioxide|A gas containing carbon and oxygen, involved in respiration and photosynthesis|Which gas is taken in as a carbon source when a green leaf photosynthesises?|Helium;Carbon monoxide;Hydrogen only|Plants use carbon dioxide in making sugars. The gas’s useful biological role does not make every concentration safe to breathe.|Explain why a gas can be useful to plants and still be unsafe in excess for people.
Photosynthesis|Light-driven production of sugars by green tissues using water and carbon dioxide|Which process removes carbon dioxide from the air while green plants make food in suitable conditions?|Burning dry wood;Animal breathing out;Rusting of iron|Carbon becomes part of plant material. Plants also respire, so their gas exchanges depend on the processes occurring rather than one simple rule for every moment.|Explain the role of carbon dioxide in plant food production.
Respiration|Cellular processes releasing usable energy from food|Which process in human cells produces carbon dioxide as a common waste of aerobic food-energy release?|Freezing water;Melting ice;Condensing clouds|Carbon dioxide moves from cells into blood and to the lungs for removal. Breathing exchanges air but is not identical to cellular respiration.|Trace how carbon dioxide produced in cells leaves a person’s body.
Combustion|Burning reactions that can release carbon dioxide from carbon-containing fuels|Which process can release carbon dioxide when charcoal burns?|Freezing of pure water;Germination without respiration;Condensation only|Combustion transfers carbon in fuel to gaseous products, often including carbon dioxide. Incomplete burning can also create dangerous carbon monoxide.|Explain why charcoal burning indoors is unsafe beyond merely producing carbon dioxide.
Greenhouse gas|A gas that absorbs and emits infrared radiation, affecting Earth’s energy balance|Which role does carbon dioxide play in the atmosphere’s heat-related energy balance?|It is only an opaque solid roof;It removes all sunlight;It is chemically identical to oxygen|A natural greenhouse effect helps keep Earth warmer than it would otherwise be. Increasing greenhouse-gas concentrations can enhance warming and disrupt climate.|Distinguish a useful natural effect from problems caused by increasing its strength.
Ventilation|Exchange of indoor air with suitable fresh air|A crowded room needs adequate fresh-air exchange. Which practice helps prevent excessive accumulation of exhaled gases?|Sealing every opening indefinitely;Adding smoke;Breathing into closed bags|Air exchange reduces buildup and can also reduce some other indoor pollutants. It does not make a dangerous confined space automatically safe to enter.|Explain why ventilation matters in an occupied classroom.
Carbon monoxide|A poisonous gas different from carbon dioxide, often associated with incomplete combustion|Which dangerous gas may be produced by incompletely burning fuel and must not be confused with carbon dioxide?|Pure oxygen;Water vapour only;Nitrogen alone|The two names refer to different substances. Burning fuel in poorly ventilated spaces can expose people to carbon monoxide, requiring prevention rather than classroom experimentation.|Explain why similar names do not mean two gases have the same effects.
Fire suppression|Reducing conditions needed for combustion to continue|A correctly selected carbon-dioxide extinguisher may suppress an appropriate fire. What purpose is being served?|Feeding a plant directly;Increasing oxygen supply;Making water drinkable|Carbon dioxide can reduce oxygen around a fire, but extinguishers have specific uses and hazards. Only trained adults should choose and operate them.|Explain why knowing a gas can suppress some fires does not authorise pupils to use an extinguisher.
Carbon storage|Retention of carbon in materials such as growing plants and soil|A tree incorporates carbon into its growing tissues. Which role is illustrated?|Instant creation of carbon from nothing;Removal of all atmospheric gases;Production of metal|Some carbon remains in biomass for a period. It can return through respiration, decay or burning, so storage is part of a cycle rather than permanent disappearance.|Explain how stored plant carbon can later return to the atmosphere.
Emission reduction|Lowering the release of a substance into the environment|Reducing unnecessary burning of waste can reduce releases of carbon dioxide and other pollutants. Which broad action is this?|Creating more smoke;Blocking all photosynthesis;Ignoring fuel use|Avoiding unnecessary burning reduces pollution at its source. Safe waste management and efficient energy use are practical supporting measures.|Give two actions that can reduce unnecessary releases from burning.''')

add(5,10,'Protecting water and keeping air cleaner','B5.2.1.5.1–2','B5.2.1.5.1–2','Direct indicator match; community actions are taught without unsafe field sampling.',
'Use pictures of waste dumping, runoff, smoke, dusty roads, clean drains and protected water containers. Create a source–pathway–effect–prevention table. Design a school poster recommending achievable actions and identify hazards that require adults or authorities.',
'No collecting sewage, entering drains, touching chemical containers or standing beside smoke or heavy traffic. Any clean-up must be adult-supervised and exclude hazardous waste.',
'''Water pollution|Contamination making water harmful or unsuitable for intended uses|Wastewater enters a stream used by a community. Which environmental problem is illustrated?|Photosynthesis;Safe storage;Seed germination|Pollution may include microbes, chemicals or excess materials. A change may be harmful even when the water still looks clear.|Explain why appearance alone cannot establish that stream water is safe.
Sewage|Wastewater that may contain human waste and disease-causing organisms|Which discharge poses a particular microbial risk if released untreated into a stream?|Clean labelled drinking water;Unopened bottled water;A dry paper label|Human-waste contamination can transmit disease. Treatment and sanitation should prevent it reaching drinking-water sources.|Explain why untreated human wastewater must be kept away from water supplies.
Runoff pollution|Movement of pollutants into water as water flows over land|Rain carries fertiliser from a field into a nearby stream. Which pathway is shown?|Sunlight reflection;Plant flowering;Water disappearing from existence|Materials on the land can travel with runoff. Appropriate use, storage and protective practices can reduce entry into water.|Explain how pollution can reach a stream even without direct dumping into it.
Oil contamination|Introduction of oil into water or soil where it can cause harm|Used oil is poured into a drain leading toward water. What pollution risk is created?|Automatic water purification;Increased oxygen production by oil;Safer drinking water|Oil can harm organisms and is difficult to remove fully. Correct collection and disposal prevent a problem that is harder to solve afterward.|Explain why prevention is preferable to trying to clean a polluted stream later.
Air pollution|Presence of harmful substances or particles in the air|Smoke from open waste burning enters a classroom. Which environmental problem occurs?|Clean-air protection;Cloud-free weather only;Safe ventilation by itself|Smoke can contain harmful particles and gases. Avoiding unnecessary burning reduces exposure rather than relying on people to tolerate it.|Explain why smoke should not be treated as harmless simply because it disperses.
Particulate matter|Small solid or liquid particles suspended in air|Dust and fine smoke particles can be breathed into the respiratory system. What general pollutant category is involved?|Only visible rocks on the ground;Pure sunlight;Water in a sealed bottle|Small particles can enter the airways. Some are too small to see individually, so lack of obvious visible dust does not prove clean air.|Explain why invisible particles can still be a health concern.
Source control|Preventing or reducing pollution where it is produced|A school prevents open burning instead of only closing windows when smoke arrives. Which approach is used?|Spreading pollution elsewhere;Ignoring the cause;Creating a new smoke source|Reducing the source protects more people and places. Downstream protective measures alone may leave the original hazard unchanged.|Explain an advantage of reducing pollution at its source.
Proper waste disposal|Handling discarded materials through appropriate collection, treatment or safe disposal routes|Rubbish is put into designated collection rather than a drain. Which practice is shown?|Deliberate water contamination;Open dumping;Uncontrolled burning|Keeping waste out of drains reduces blockages and movement into water. Hazardous materials need specialised handling beyond ordinary litter collection.|Explain why pupils should not collect unknown chemical waste during a clean-up.
Ventilation|Exchange of air to reduce indoor accumulation of pollutants when outdoor conditions are suitable|A classroom receives suitable fresh air rather than trapping indoor fumes. Which practice is shown?|Sealing in smoke;Adding dust;Burning fuel inside|Air exchange can help indoor conditions, but outdoor smoke may require additional source-control and school safety decisions. Ventilation is not a licence for indoor burning.|Explain why ventilation does not make indoor charcoal burning an acceptable classroom activity.
Community responsibility|Coordinated actions by people and authorities to protect shared resources|Families, schools and local authorities cooperate to protect a water source. Which idea is illustrated?|One person ignoring everyone else;Private ownership of all air;Unplanned dumping|Shared sources can be affected by many users. Cooperation, reporting hazards and safe routines protect people beyond one household.|Give one suitable pupil action and one action requiring adults or authorities.''')

add(5,11,'How plant structures suit their functions','B5.2.2.1.1','B5.2.2.1.1','Direct indicator match; this grade emphasises structure–function relationships rather than labels alone.',
'Observe leaf shape, veins, stem support, branching roots and flowers from safe specimens or pictures. For each, complete “structure → function → benefit”. Produce a labelled drawing with two written explanations, and compare plants without claiming every species has identical structures.',
'Avoid unknown sap and thorns. Do not taste plant parts or uproot protected plants. Use teacher-selected specimens.',
'''Leaf blade|The broad expanded part of many leaves that exposes surface to light|Which leaf structure commonly provides a broad area for capturing light?|A seed jar;A narrow root hair only;A fruit stone only|A broad surface can intercept light, although leaf shapes vary with species and conditions. Surface area is one adaptation, not a universal identical design.|Explain how a broad leaf surface can support photosynthesis.
Leaf veins|Transport and supporting structures running through a leaf|A network within a leaf carries materials and helps support the blade. What is it?|Seed coats;Petal colours;Loose soil|Veins connect with transport pathways in the stem and help distribute water and products. Their arrangement can also provide physical support.|State two roles of a leaf’s vein system.
Stomata|Small openings involved in leaf gas exchange and water-vapour loss|Through which structures can carbon dioxide enter many leaves?|Large fruit pits;Wooden stakes;Seed labels|Gas exchange supports photosynthesis and respiration. Openings also allow water loss, so regulation is important.|Explain one benefit and one water-related cost of leaf openings.
Root hairs|Small root-surface extensions increasing contact area with soil water|Which structures help a root absorb water efficiently by increasing surface area?|Flower petals;Fruit pulp;Stem thorns only|Many small extensions contact water around soil particles. They differ from large lateral roots even though both contribute to a root system’s effectiveness.|Explain the link between surface area and absorption.
Branching roots|A spreading root arrangement reaching more soil and providing anchorage|A root system extends in several directions through the soil. Which structural feature helps it reach resources?|A single detached petal;A paper label;A cloud layer|A wider spread explores more soil and supports the plant. The arrangement still needs suitable water and oxygen conditions to function well.|Explain two benefits of spreading roots.
Stem support|The role of a stem in holding leaves and reproductive structures in useful positions|A firm stem raises leaves into better light. Which role is demonstrated?|Only making pollen;Only storing rain outside the plant;Only dispersing seeds by wind|Positioning leaves can improve light access and hold flowers for reproduction. Support works together with transport, not instead of it.|Explain why support and transport must operate together.
Xylem|Plant tissue mainly transporting water and dissolved minerals from roots|Which internal pathway carries much of the absorbed water toward leaves?|Petal pigment;Seed coat only;Antennae|The transport system connects roots and aerial organs. Naming the tissue does not mean the stem contains ordinary open drinking straws of the same scale as the model.|Trace a water pathway through the plant and state one limit of a straw model.
Phloem|Plant tissue transporting sugars and other organic materials between sources and sinks|Which tissue moves food made in leaves toward growing or storage parts?|Only external bark colour;Pollen grains;Root soil particles|Sugar can move from producing regions to places needing or storing it. This differs from the principal water-and-mineral role of xylem.|Distinguish the main transported materials in xylem and phloem.
Petal|A flower part that can help attract pollinators in many species|A brightly coloured flower structure attracts visiting insects. Which part is likely involved?|Root hair;Main underground root;Leaf vein only|Colour or scent may help attract pollinators, but not every flower relies on bright petals. Wind-pollinated plants can have less conspicuous flowers.|Explain why bright petals are useful in some flowers but not necessary in all flowers.
Seed dispersal|Movement of seeds away from the parent plant|A fruit’s structure helps an animal carry seeds away. Which function is supported?|Only leaf gas exchange;Only water absorption;Only stem stiffening|Dispersal can reduce local competition and help reach new suitable places. Success still depends on later conditions for survival and germination.|Explain how a fruit’s structure can benefit seeds after they leave the parent.''')

add(5,12,'Comparing bean and maize germination','B5.2.2.1.2','B5.2.2.1.2','Direct indicator match. Common bean and maize observations are used without generalising to every bean species or every germination pattern.',
'Set up several viable common-bean and maize seeds under similar suitable moisture, air and warmth. Record daily drawings and emergence dates. Compare cotyledons, food reserves, first root, protected shoot and the position of seed leaves during early growth. Use photographs if time is too short to complete observation.',
'No tasting treated or experimental seeds. Avoid mould and excessive water. Do not claim predicted observations were actually measured; label photographs and supplied data clearly.',
'''Cotyledon|A seed leaf whose number and role help distinguish bean and maize seeds|What is the general name for each of the two fleshy seed leaves in a common bean?|Petal;Root hair;Pollen grain|Common bean has two cotyledons, which hold important food reserves. Maize has one cotyledon with a different role in obtaining stored food.|Compare cotyledon number in common bean and maize.
Two cotyledons|The usual cotyledon count in a common bean embryo|How many seed leaves are normally present in a common bean seed?|One cotyledon;No embryo;Ten cotyledons|Two seed leaves are characteristic of this example. The count should not be confused with the number of later foliage leaves.|Explain why counting later leaves is not the same as counting seed leaves.
One cotyledon|The usual cotyledon count in a maize embryo|How many cotyledons does a maize embryo normally have?|Two cotyledons;No cotyledon;Four cotyledons|Maize’s single cotyledon is specialised in transferring food from reserves to the growing embryo. It does not mean the mature plant has only one leaf.|Explain why maize having one cotyledon does not limit it to one adult leaf.
Endosperm|Food-storage tissue that remains important in a maize grain|Which tissue provides much of the stored food used by a germinating maize embryo?|Outer soil;Flower petals;Leaf hairs|Maize retains substantial reserves in endosperm, while common bean stores much in cotyledons. Both use reserves before photosynthesis supplies enough food.|Compare the main reserve locations in these two seeds.
Radicle|The embryonic root emerging early in germination|Which young structure commonly appears early in both germinating bean and maize?|Petal;Fruit stalk;Pollen tube|Root development begins establishing uptake and anchorage. Later root arrangements may differ between the plants.|Explain why an early similarity does not mean both mature root systems will be identical.
Plumule|The embryonic shoot that develops toward foliage|Which part of the embryo develops into the young shoot in both plants?|Soil particle;Seed container;Petal colour|The shoot eventually supports photosynthetic leaves. Its emergence is distinct from the initial root’s role.|Compare the roles of the young root and shoot during establishment.
Coleoptile|A protective sheath surrounding the young grass shoot in maize|Which structure protects the emerging maize shoot as it grows upward?|A bean flower petal;A root nodule only;A fruit’s ripe flesh|The sheath protects delicate shoot tissues during emergence. It is a feature of the maize example, not a second cotyledon.|Explain why the protective sheath should not be counted as another cotyledon.
Epigeal germination|A pattern in which cotyledons are carried above the ground during emergence|Common bean seedlings often lift their seed leaves above the soil. Which pattern is shown?|Hypogeal germination;Pollination;Seed dispersal|Elongation below the cotyledons lifts them in this example. The pattern should be confirmed by observations rather than assumed for every plant called a bean.|Describe the observation needed to identify this pattern.
Hypogeal germination|A pattern in which cotyledon position remains below the ground during emergence|In maize, the cotyledon remains associated with the grain below ground as the shoot emerges. Which pattern fits?|Epigeal germination;Fruit ripening;Combustion|The seed leaf is not lifted as in the common-bean example. Shoot emergence does not require every seed part to move above the soil.|Explain why an above-ground maize shoot does not mean its cotyledon was lifted above ground.
Fair comparison|An investigation using comparable conditions while examining a chosen difference|Bean and maize seeds are compared with similar moisture, air and temperature, using several seeds each. Which design feature is strengthened?|Changing all conditions;Using only one guessed result;Mixing up labels|Comparable conditions reduce confounding, and several seeds reveal variation. Real observations should be recorded rather than replaced by an expected textbook sequence.|Name two conditions to keep similar and explain why several seeds are useful.''')
